<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Germanic peoples</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Germanic</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Germanic peoples were speakers of Germanic languages in northern and central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. They were frequent opponents and later federates of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, whose western half fell to Germanic kingdoms; the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> among them founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a>. Their westward movements are also linked to pressures from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huns">Huns</a>, who are associated with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/germanic-peoples.jpg" alt="Germanic peoples" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The original uploader was Wolpertinger at German Wikipedia. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Germanische-ratsversammlung_1-1250x715.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Germanic peoples were tribal groups who lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-europe">Northern Europe</a> during <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/classical-antiquity">Classical antiquity</a> and the Early Middle Ages. Scholars generally agree that Germanic languages can be spoken of from about 500 BCE. Archaeologists usually associate the earliest clearly identifiable Germanic-speaking peoples with the Jastorf culture of central and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> and southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> from the 6th to 1st centuries BCE. Some scholars call for the term's abandonment as a modern construct, since it implies a common identity for which there is little evidence, while others defend it on the ground of a common language. The name Germani, whose etymology is unknown, was first used for a large category of peoples distinct from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gauls">Gauls</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scythians">Scythians</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a>, around 55 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbri">Cimbri</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutones">Teutones</a> and Ambrones, whom Caesar later classified as Germanic, migrated late in the 2nd century BCE and defeated several Roman armies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cimbrian-war">Cimbrian War</a> of 113-101 BCE before being defeated. Under Augustus the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> tried to conquer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elbe">Elbe</a> but withdrew after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-teutoburg-forest">Battle of the Teutoburg Forest</a> in 9 CE, and thereafter they managed the frontier with the fortified <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limes-germanicus">Limes Germanicus</a>. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marcomannic-wars">Marcomannic Wars</a> of 166 to 180, new groupings appear, such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/goths">Goths</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and Alemanni.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/migration-period">Migration Period</a>, traditionally dated from 375 to 568, Germanic peoples entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> and established kingdoms within the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-roman-empire">Western Roman Empire</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> became the most powerful, and Merovingian Frankia was divided into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrasia">Austrasia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neustria">Neustria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>. Pepin the Short became king in 751 and founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-dynasty">Carolingian dynasty</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> conquered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-lombards">Lombards</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Bavarians</a> and was crowned Roman emperor in 800.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Roman sources portrayed Germanic life as more primitive than archaeology shows: it has revealed a complex society and economy. Agriculture was organised around villages rather than the large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/villa-estate">villae</a> rusticae of the Roman provinces, and only about 30 percent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germania">Germania</a> was covered in forest. Despite <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a>'s claim that the Germani had little iron, smiths were skilful. Roman sources report decisions made in a popular assembly, the thing, alongside kings and war leaders.</p><p>The peoples shared a native script, the runes, whose earliest form has 24 characters, and a common poetic tradition. Most continental Germanic peoples and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxons</a> converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> during Late Antiquity, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxons">Saxons</a> and Scandinavians much later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Germanic peoples” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Franks were a confederation of <b>Germanic peoples</b> who appear in Roman sources from the third century on the lower Rhine, and by the fifth century groups of Salian Franks had settled in the north of Roman Gaul, often as allies of the empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-franks">Kingdom of the Franks · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Sarmatian dominance was undermined in the third century CE by the arrival of <b>Germanic peoples</b> from the north-west.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sarmatians">Sarmatians · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>The Huns who invaded Europe in the late fourth and fifth centuries may have been connected with the Xiongnu, though the link remains debated; their pressure helped to drive <b>Germanic peoples</b> across the Rhine and Danube frontiers and contributed to the crisis of the Western Roman Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/xiongnu-empire">Xiongnu Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Norse religion was the Scandinavian branch of the religion of the <b>Germanic peoples</b>, which had developed over many centuries before the Viking Age.</blockquote><a href="/religion/norse-religion">Norse religion · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Some 400 manuscripts of Old English survive: a small corpus, but one of the most substantial bodies of literature preserved among the early <b>Germanic peoples</b>.</blockquote><a href="/language/old-english">Old English · Literature &amp; Learning</a></li><li><blockquote>Tacitus, describing the <b>Germanic peoples</b> in 98 CE, noted that they chose their kings for noble birth and their war leaders for courage, and that the chiefs decided lesser matters while the assembled warriors decided great ones; Spanish chroniclers such as Gonzalo Fernández de Oviedo described the caciques…</blockquote><a href="/government/chiefdom">Chiefdom · Origins</a></li></ul></section>
